00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:21.460
down and use it as a resource,

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:24.340
not just as a consumable for astronauts, but as a fuel.

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:27.780
So you could go there, fuel up a spacecraft, and come back,

00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:28.940
or maybe go on to Mars.

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:29.420
Really?

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:29.940
We'll talk.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:30.920
Tell us a little bit more about this.

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:36.400
Yeah, so we try to think about drilling as the main kind of driver for the vehicle.

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:40.080
I have to design around this pretty big drill, something that's, you know, bigger than

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:42.780
an inch diameter, you know, something that would be pretty hard to do by hand.

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:45.580
Go down a few feet deep into the ground.

00:15:45.760 --> 00:15:51.650
And so we thought of this really slow, strong kind of machine to do this heavy task.

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:54.030
So we designed it kind of like a doughnut around this drill.

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:56.560
You can see that stand-in drill there.

00:15:56.700 --> 00:15:56.980
Yes.

00:15:57.360 --> 00:15:59.960
That's kind of to simulate the maths of it so that it feels...

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:03.460
like it's there for the rover while it drives but then so you know Scott's

00:16:03.460 --> 00:16:07.020
driving and now you can see that it's this is its kind of normal pace it's

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:10.840
driving out now. This fast? This is about as fast as it gets yeah but it turns out

00:16:10.840 --> 00:16:15.040
it's fast enough to get the mission done. I guess one of the nice visible things

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:19.040
about it is the suspension I mean you're seeing this this kind of the all the

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:22.080
hardware that connects between the wheels we kind of call these the

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:25.680
shoulders where it rocks around those shoulder points and then this beam

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:27.340
and connects the two sides together.

00:16:27.540 --> 00:16:29.020
So that's all passive joints.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:31.760
and it's worked out really nicely for us.

00:16:31.960 --> 00:16:34.650
And one aspect we haven't talked about so far

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:37.070
is the way it navigates in this total darkness.

00:16:37.710 --> 00:16:40.020
You know, you can't just sit there and take pictures.

00:16:40.260 --> 00:16:41.920
There's no light basically coming.

00:16:42.580 --> 00:16:45.040
And it turns out if you want to take really far shots,

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:46.260
you know, see far in the distance,

00:16:47.560 --> 00:16:49.450
flash, illumination, it doesn't work out very well.

00:16:49.710 --> 00:16:52.770
So we've been experimenting with some laser systems.

00:16:52.950 --> 00:16:55.430
So you're pointing lasers, you know,

00:16:55.490 --> 00:16:56.950
shining out into the surroundings

00:16:56.950 --> 00:16:58.130
and seeing what bounces back.

00:16:58.130 --> 00:16:59.910
So these are two different scanners.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:04.540
we've been experimenting with. This one's kind of been using as a, it's almost like

00:17:04.540 --> 00:17:09.240
a virtual bumper. It paints a laser line, a stripe out in front of it, a few feet in

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:14.100
front of the nose, and makes sure there aren't any big obstacles in the way. And if they

00:17:14.100 --> 00:17:18.940
are, the software sees that and responds, finds a better path. And then this is a much more

00:17:18.940 --> 00:17:23.100
complex device, it's really nice, called the Tridar. This one's actually getting, it

00:17:23.100 --> 00:17:29.300
can get bigger scans, like really detailed, nice 3D meshes, these 3D maps of the terrain

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:29.680
around.

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:32.860
around the rover and then it can choose the best paths through.

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:35.140
So bro, is this a test model or prototype?

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:40.160
Yeah, exactly. It's kind of testing out an idea for, you know, how to do this kind of drilling and driving

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:42.620
in these cold, cold craters, dark craters.

00:17:42.880 --> 00:17:43.380
Like here?

00:17:43.610 --> 00:17:44.690
Like here, a little bit, yeah.

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:46.170
Why it's so cold?

00:17:46.270 --> 00:17:46.670
It is.

00:17:46.930 --> 00:17:52.550
So basically, you know, as far as the background, you know, that NASA's Human Robotic Systems program

00:17:52.550 --> 00:17:54.530
is the one we're developing this for.

00:17:54.650 --> 00:17:58.910
They came to us with the kind of the needs, you know, what the rover would need to do.

00:17:58.910 --> 00:17:59.970
And then, you know,

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.880
And so we're at Carnegie Mellon's Field Robotics Center.

00:18:03.100 --> 00:18:06.020
And so we came up with the concept, kind of in coordination

00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:07.780
with them, and then built it last summer.

