Thank you, bro.

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:42.800
Check it out. You see some guys in spacesuits, but not all of these spacesuits are NASA spacesuits.

00:18:43.130 --> 00:18:46.670
Some of these suits are props. Right, props from a Hollywood prop house.

00:18:47.110 --> 00:18:50.230
Let me try to explain. All NASA spacesuits are pressurized,

00:18:50.540 --> 00:18:52.840
which makes them basically their own self-contained spacecraft.

00:18:53.520 --> 00:18:57.100
This pressurization is great when you're in a 1-6 gravity environment like the moon,

00:18:57.530 --> 00:18:59.970
but you don't need to have all the suits pressurized for this testing.

00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:02.840
So NASA had some unpressurized suits made in Hollywood.

00:19:03.440 --> 00:19:05.380
These suits are much lighter than the real suits,

00:19:05.540 --> 00:19:07.980
but can still help test ergonomics and ease of movement.

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:10.040
A couple of other benefits of the Hollywood suit

00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:13.060
is that they'll be more comparable to the actual weight and feel of suits

00:19:13.060 --> 00:19:15.020
once an astronaut gets in one-sixth gravity,

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:17.800
so astronauts will feel similar to the way it feels on the moon.

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:21.100
And the suits won't have to be recharged like a pressurized suit does,

00:19:21.420 --> 00:19:23.100
so work won't have to stop as often.

00:19:23.630 --> 00:19:26.170
Pretty cool, huh? Those suits are amazing.

00:19:26.810 --> 00:19:29.970
But obviously, real prototype spacesuits were tested as...

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:34.260
well. I'm going to throw it back out to Johnny again. He's with space suit technician Bill

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:37.740
Welch to find out more about this new suit and how it's going to work on the moon.

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:47.330
So I just saw you in the field wearing the suit. Can we talk about the suit?

00:19:47.510 --> 00:19:53.450
Sure. The suits that we're running today were future lunar and Mars surface suits, test

00:19:53.450 --> 00:19:58.510
beds actually. We're trying to take and incorporate some changes into the suits that they didn't

00:19:58.510 --> 00:19:59.550
have during the Apollo days.

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:04.690
which will enable them to be able to walk on the moon's surface rather than hop, as they did before.

00:20:04.930 --> 00:20:09.500
The hopping, while worked better than walking, was fatiguing for the astronaut.

00:20:09.740 --> 00:20:12.200
It was very hard on the calves and the ankles.

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:18.920
So in order to take and alleviate that, we've incorporated bearings into the hips and into the ankles of these suits

00:20:18.920 --> 00:20:22.130
to allow more freedom of movement.

00:20:22.700 --> 00:20:27.730
So we have a bearing here, here, and here, which allows for good freedom of movement.

00:20:27.850 --> 00:20:29.890
Another thing that they don't have that they didn't have

00:20:30.030 --> 00:20:33.190
on the A7L, which was the moon suit, was the waist bearing.

00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:35.020
I don't know if you noticed while I was walking,

00:20:35.220 --> 00:20:36.880
but there was a lot of swivel in the hip.

00:20:37.280 --> 00:20:38.480
That's really big.

00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:40.200
That makes a big difference as far as comfort,

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:42.000
being able to move freely in the suit.

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:44.740
Is it really difficult to get into a suit like this?

00:20:44.900 --> 00:20:45.420
Well, no.

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:49.770
The cooling garment, see if I can take and grab one here.

00:20:50.340 --> 00:20:51.000
Excuse me, folks.

00:20:54.730 --> 00:20:55.750
This is the cooling garment.

00:20:55.830 --> 00:20:56.910
This is what we wear on the inside.

00:20:57.220 --> 00:20:58.860
This is being flown right now.

00:20:59.200 --> 00:20:59.980
This is used in...

00:21:00.030 --> 00:21:04.610
EMU up on shuttling station. What we do is you can see all the tubing running

00:21:04.610 --> 00:21:08.280
through it. We pump cold water through that tubing to keep me cool inside the

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:12.460
suit so I don't overheat. No kidding. Yeah and as you can tell it's fairly slick.

00:21:13.060 --> 00:21:18.740
It's a tight-fitting suit so we'll take and we'll roll this ladder up behind.

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:23.280
I'll take and sit on the seat. I'll hook up my water lines which come off from

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:27.890
right there and then hook on to right here. Okay and once that is done basically

00:21:27.890 --> 00:21:29.890
what they'll do is they'll just go, my suit techs will take.

00:21:30.030 --> 00:21:34.670
grab hold of the toes of the suit and pull them forward and I just drop right in.

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:35.010
Really?

00:21:35.350 --> 00:21:38.370
And then once I'm in the suit they'll take and finish up my connections, they'll put in

00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:43.730
my ear buds so I'll have my radio communications, my microphones are already in place, they'll

00:21:43.730 --> 00:21:50.150
hook up my shoulder pads, at that point they'll close the back door, put on my gloves, get

00:21:50.150 --> 00:21:53.730
the air flow started, put on my helmet, pressurize me up and out the door I go.

00:21:54.670 --> 00:21:59.970
So basically the concept of these suits is to be able to take in either A or A or A.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:03.280
leave them outside the vehicle and be able to put the back in.

00:22:03.480 --> 00:22:07.180
And we are just out here trying to provide the best product possible

00:22:07.180 --> 00:22:11.630
for the future of lunar surface and Mars surface exploration.

00:22:11.830 --> 00:22:15.500
So this is basically like your own little space shuttle.

00:22:15.740 --> 00:22:18.860
Indeed it is. It's our own little spaceship, just like the EMU out in space.

00:22:18.860 --> 00:22:24.880
Once they leave out of the airlock on the space station, they are in a spaceship.

00:22:25.060 --> 00:22:27.960
They have their telemetry, they have their batteries, they have their cooling, they have their air.

00:22:28.100 --> 00:22:29.960
They have everything. They even have the cable.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:32.920
should they come disconnected from the space station,

00:22:33.040 --> 00:22:34.480
to be able to drive themselves back.

00:22:34.660 --> 00:22:37.630
They have a little gaseous nitrogen rocket motor

00:22:37.630 --> 00:22:40.310
that is hooked onto them and they're able to take

00:22:40.310 --> 00:22:42.350
and drive themselves back in an emergency situation.

00:22:42.650 --> 00:22:44.850
So yes, they definitely have their own little spaceship

00:22:44.850 --> 00:22:46.810
and that's what we're working on is having

00:22:46.810 --> 00:22:49.510
your own little moon-Mars rover.

00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:51.980
Well, it's been a pleasure talking to you.

00:22:52.100 --> 00:22:52.960
Thank you, sir, right?

00:22:53.140 --> 00:22:53.260
Take care.

00:22:53.360 --> 00:22:54.080
My most definitely.

